{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Symptom\n\nFive tests in `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_atomic_completion_close.py` fail locally for any\ndeveloper whose git config sets `safe.bareRepository = explicit`. They pass under git's default\n(`all`), which is what CI uses, so **CI can never exercise this path**.\n\nAll five fail identically — not an assertion, a `CalledProcessError` from the test helper:\n`Command '['git', '-C', '.../tickets-remote.git', 'ls-tree', ...]' returned non-zero exit\nstatus 128`.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`git` refuses to **discover** a bare repository via `-C` / cwd when `safe.bareRepository=explicit`;\nit accepts one named by a top-level `--git-dir`. Exit 128 is a refusal to open the repository at\nall, *before* any ref lookup:\n\n```\ngit -C <bare>  -> fatal: cannot use bare repository '<bare>' (safe.bareRepository is 'explicit')\ngit --git-dir <bare>  -> exit 0\n```\n\nThe offending helper is `_git` at `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_atomic_completion_close.py:40-46`,\nwhich hardcodes `[\"git\", \"-C\", str(repo), *args]`. The bare remote is built in `_ticket_remote`\n(:97-117) and read at **:401, :402, :415, :423, :435, :455, :493, :576** — eight sites across the\nfive failing tests.\n\nTwo claims in the original report are wrong: the `refs/heads/tickets` ref EXISTS (git never gets\nas far as looking it up), and git 2.55.0 is a red herring — `safe.bareRepository` shipped in git\n2.38.0 with these exact semantics.\n\n## This is a recurrence of a class already fixed\n\nClosed ticket `3476-4472-3407-4246` (`embolismic-adverse-dragon`) records the policy verbatim:\n\"the supported test suite must run both locally and in CI. **Treat agents dismissing reproducible\nlocal test failures as environmental noise as an anti-pattern.**\" It converted 13 files from\n`git -C <bare>` to `git --git-dir <bare>` and added the held-out oracle\n`tests/integration/test_bare_repository_fixture_portability_heldout.py`.\n\n**Why this file escaped the guard:** that oracle pins a **hardcoded allowlist** of 3 fixture nodes\n(`_FIXTURE_NODES`). `test_atomic_completion_close.py` was added on 2026-08-25 by commit\n`1cb1cdd25aff` — four days *after* the guard landed — and reintroduced the banned construct. There\nis no static sweep either, so the guard only checks three files that were already fixed and cannot\nfail when a new file reintroduces the defect. **That allowlist is why this is the second\noccurrence, and leaving it is why there would be a third.**\n\n## Plan\n\n1. **Point fix.** Add a sibling helper `_bare_git(remote, *args)` that addresses the bare remote\n   with a top-level `--git-dir`, and route only the bare-remote call sites through it. `_git` is\n   also used on **non-bare** worktrees (`tracker`, `writer`, `tmp_path` for `init`/`clone`), so a\n   blanket `-C` -> `--git-dir` swap is wrong. This is the shape ticket 3476 already established in\n   the integration fixtures (`_bare_git` beside `_git`).\n2. **Close the oracle gap with a static sweep, in the repo's established gate idiom.** Add\n   `scripts/check_bare_repository_discovery.py` alongside `check_raw_git_writes.py` and\n   `check_wall_clock_asserts.py`, wired into `make lint`, carrying\n   `# mechanism-ok: ci_gate scripts/check_bare_repository_discovery.py — <reason>` in its opening\n   lines, since the mechanism-delta ratchet fails on any new gate script without one. It AST-sweeps\n   every `tests/**/*.py` and, per module, derives the identifiers bound to a bare repository:\n   - the last path operand after a `--bare` token (`git init --bare <dir>`, and\n     `git clone --bare <src> <dst>`, whose *source* must not be marked);\n   - the directory the call runs in when the flag takes no operand (`_git(remote, \"init\", \"-q\",\n     \"--bare\")`, the shape the already-fixed integration fixtures use);\n   - a name bound by a bare-repository factory: a `bare`-named creator taking only paths\n     (`init_bare_remote(remote)`), or a same-module helper that creates a bare repository **and\n     returns it** — tracked by return POSITION, so `return remote, writer` marks only the first\n     element and a helper returning the *worktree* marks nothing, and applied to the **caller's\n     binding name** read off the assignment, so a rename (`origin, scribe = _ticket_remote(...)`)\n     is still covered;\n   - the **argument passed at a parameter position** that the callee marks bare in its own body,\n     which is what covers `_publish_ticket_store(repo, remote)` by dataflow rather than by the\n     caller happening to spell the name the same way.\n   Marking is otherwise module-scoped by identifier, which covers the same flows a second way.\n   Then flag any `-C` invocation targeting a marked name — direct, or through a module-local helper\n   that forwards its first parameter to `-C`. Two calls are **exempt**: one carrying the inline\n   `-c safe.bareRepository=all` opt-in, the deliberate escape ticket 3476 preserved in\n   `tests/scripts/test_reconcile_bridge_push_retry.py` (it passes under the strict config today);\n   and the **creating** call, since `git -C <dir> init --bare` runs before `<dir>` is a repository.\n3. **Retire the allowlist.** Delete `_FIXTURE_NODES` from\n   `tests/integration/test_bare_repository_fixture_portability_heldout.py`; it becomes the oracle\n   for the sweep — asserting the checker reports nothing tree-wide, self-testing the detector on\n   synthetic offending / renamed-binding / escaped modules so it cannot rot into a tautology, and\n   pinning the git behaviour the rule encodes (`-C` on a bare repo exits 128 naming\n   `safe.bareRepository`; `--git-dir` exits 0).\n4. Fix every site the sweep flags, so `make lint` is green on landing: 12 sites in 2 files — 8 in\n   the file under fix and 4 in `tests/interfaces/test_tracker_footprint_cli.py`, whose\n   `test_fresh_clone_json_exposes_allocation_without_a_policy_verdict` and\n   `test_invalid_configured_branch_cleans_only_its_temporary_child` fail under the strict config\n   today for exactly the same reason.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `env GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=<file setting safe.bareRepository=explicit> GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1 pytest tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_atomic_completion_close.py` exits 0 with 17 passed (today: 5 failed, 12 passed).\n- [ ] The same file still passes under default git config (17 passed), and the full `pytest tests/interfaces/lifecycle` run still passes.\n- [ ] `tests/interfaces/test_tracker_footprint_cli.py` passes under the strict config too (today: 2 failed, 5 passed) and under the default config.\n- [ ] `python scripts/check_bare_repository_discovery.py` exits 0 on the fixed tree and is run by `make lint`; reintroducing the `git -C <bare-repo>` construct anywhere under `tests/` makes it exit non-zero. Demonstrated by temporarily reverting one call site, observing the guard go RED, and restoring it.\n- [ ] The guard's coverage is derived from the tree, not from a hand-maintained file allowlist: `_FIXTURE_NODES` is gone, and a new bare-fixture test using `git -C <bare>` fails the gate with no registration step.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass, including the module-size, complexity and mechanism-delta ratchets, with the new gate justified by an inline `# mechanism-ok: ci_gate ...` marker rather than a hand-edited baseline.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Reproduce RED first: `env GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=$STRICT GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1 python -m pytest tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_atomic_completion_close.py -q` must show `5 failed, 12 passed` before the fix and `17 passed` after; the same for `tests/interfaces/test_tracker_footprint_cli.py` (`2 failed, 5 passed` -> `7 passed`).\n- Default-config runs of both files, and the directory regression `python -m pytest tests/interfaces/lifecycle -q`.\n- Guard: `python scripts/check_bare_repository_discovery.py` and `python -m pytest tests/integration/test_bare_repository_fixture_portability_heldout.py -q`, plus a mutation run with one call site reverted to `_git(remote, ...)` to prove the gate goes RED.\n- Ratchets and gates: `make lint` (module-size, `check_complexity_baseline.py --check`, `check_mechanism_delta.py --check`) and `make typecheck`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788485074970443001,"uuid":"b624c2db-d687-4356-beeb-923450126bb9"}